  "query_preview": "..Isolated showers and thunderstorms have fired up\nacross portions of the High Country this afternoon. Storm motion\nis relatively slow, so this will increase the potential for flash\nflooding, especially for storms that continue to back build.\nThere's decent instability across our CWA this afternoon, but with\nlittle to no shear, we aren't anticipating many, if any, severe\nthunderstorms. \n\nAs for the synoptic pattern...A strong ridge will continue to \nbuild across the Four Corners through the next couple of days \nbefore the high slightly wobbles eastward. Even with a slight \nshift in the general placement of this pressure system, above \nnormal afternoon high temperatures and daily thunderstorm chances \nwill remain through the entire forecast period. We are also\nexpecting an influx of additional moisture from the southeast\nstarting tomorrow and lasting through the first half of next week\nat least. Then we could see a slight downward trend in daily\nprecipitation chances, although we aren't expecting to \"dry out\". \nAn Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect for elevations below \n4000' at the Grand Canyon and for Marble and Glen Canyon through \nSunday evening. Although these are the only areas where we have a \nheat product in effect, all lower elevation locations will be \nexperiencing hot daytime highs through the next several days, so \navoid strenuous activity during the middle of the afternoon and \nstay hydrated! As for precipitation, greatest chances will be \nacross the higher terrain each afternoon/early evening. Flash \nflooding will likely be the main concern with all the recent \nrainfall across our CWA.",
